1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,590
Multiply one by i.

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:05,353
You get i. Multiply by i again: minus one.

3
00:00:05,353 --> 00:00:07,150
Again: minus i.

4
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:08,882
Again: back to one.

5
00:00:08,882 --> 00:00:15,444
Plot those four points and something beautiful appears: multiplying by i is simply a quarter turn.

6
00:00:15,667 --> 00:00:19,145
Complex numbers are really about lengths and turns.

7
00:00:19,145 --> 00:00:23,801
See them that way, and powers and roots that look impossible become easy.

8
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:34,046
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 3: Complex Numbers.

9
00:00:36,433 --> 00:00:37,515
Four parts.

10
00:00:37,515 --> 00:00:40,133
The polar form, length and angle.

11
00:00:40,133 --> 00:00:44,208
De Moivre's theorem, for powers and for roots.

12
00:00:44,208 --> 00:00:46,174
The cube roots of unity.

13
00:00:46,174 --> 00:00:49,573
And Euler's formula, which ties it all to the number e.

14
00:00:53,933 --> 00:01:02,867
Plot z equals x plus i y as the point x, y on the Argand diagram: real axis across, imaginary axis up.

15
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:04,560
Now describe it differently.

16
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:11,148
Its distance from the origin is the modulus, r, root of x squared plus y squared.

17
00:01:11,148 --> 00:01:15,202
Its angle from the positive real axis is the argument, theta.

18
00:01:15,433 --> 00:01:24,484
Since x is r cos theta and y is r sin theta, z equals r times cos theta plus i sin theta.

19
00:01:24,484 --> 00:01:25,747
The polar form.

20
00:01:25,747 --> 00:01:26,756
Length and angle.

21
00:01:26,967 --> 00:01:28,674
Move the point and watch.

22
00:01:28,674 --> 00:01:37,879
The principal argument always lies between minus one eighty and plus one eighty degrees: anticlockwise positive, clockwise negative.

23
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:40,586
The calculator trap.

24
00:01:40,586 --> 00:01:48,346
Inverse tan of y over x cannot tell minus one minus i from one plus i: it gives the same angle.

25
00:01:48,346 --> 00:01:53,919
So find the acute angle alpha first, then fix the quadrant from the picture.

26
00:01:53,919 --> 00:01:56,745
Second quadrant: pi minus alpha.

27
00:01:56,745 --> 00:01:59,831
Third: minus, pi minus alpha.

28
00:01:59,831 --> 00:02:01,464
Fourth: minus alpha.

29
00:02:02,667 --> 00:02:03,906
Four conversions.

30
00:02:03,906 --> 00:02:07,967
Each time: find r, find the acute angle, then fix the quadrant.

31
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:13,371
One plus i root three: r two, alpha sixty, first quadrant.

32
00:02:13,371 --> 00:02:15,156
Theta is sixty degrees.

33
00:02:15,367 --> 00:02:22,296
Minus one plus i: second quadrant, so one eighty minus forty five, one thirty five.

34
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,827
Minus root three minus i: third quadrant, so minus, one eighty minus thirty: minus one fifty.

35
00:02:31,033 --> 00:02:35,764
And one minus i: fourth quadrant, minus forty five degrees.

36
00:02:37,767 --> 00:02:40,177
Here is why polar form matters.

37
00:02:40,177 --> 00:02:43,042
Multiply two complex numbers.

38
00:02:43,042 --> 00:02:49,097
Expand, and the brackets turn out to be exactly the compound angle formulas.

39
00:02:49,097 --> 00:02:55,399
So the product has length r one times r two, and angle theta one plus theta two.

40
00:02:55,399 --> 00:02:57,714
Multiplying stretches and rotates.

41
00:02:57,933 --> 00:03:02,735
And dividing does the opposite: divide the lengths, subtract the angles.

42
00:03:03,967 --> 00:03:06,828
Use the division rule, with no rationalising.

43
00:03:07,033 --> 00:03:14,091
The top has modulus root two and argument forty five; the bottom has modulus two and argument minus sixty.

44
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:22,181
Divide the moduli and subtract the arguments: modulus one over root two, argument one hundred and five degrees.

45
00:03:27,767 --> 00:03:34,487
If multiplying adds angles, then raising to the n th power multiplies the angle by n.

46
00:03:34,487 --> 00:03:44,917
That is De Moivre's theorem: cos theta plus i sin theta, to the n, equals cos n theta plus i sin n theta.

47
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:47,855
With a modulus, r becomes r to the n.

48
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:54,983
The proof for whole numbers is by induction: each extra factor adds another theta to the angle.

49
00:03:54,983 --> 00:04:00,986
It also holds for negative integers, and for fractional powers, where it gives several values.

50
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:03,048
Picture it.

51
00:04:03,048 --> 00:04:09,851
Each power turns by the same angle and scales by the same factor, so successive powers trace a spiral.

52
00:04:11,067 --> 00:04:15,157
Expanding one plus i to the tenth would take eleven terms.

53
00:04:15,157 --> 00:04:16,576
Use polar form instead.

54
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:18,899
Root two, forty five degrees.

55
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:25,110
Raise the modulus to the tenth and multiply the angle by ten: four fifty degrees.

56
00:04:25,333 --> 00:04:29,304
Four fifty is three sixty plus ninety: thirty two i.

57
00:04:31,333 --> 00:04:32,050
Another power.

58
00:04:32,567 --> 00:04:34,641
Modulus two, argument thirty.

59
00:04:34,867 --> 00:04:44,484
Two to the seven is one two eight; seven times thirty is two ten degrees, in the third quadrant, where cosine and sine are both negative.

60
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:50,984
So minus sixty four, root three plus i. Watch those signs: that is where marks go.

61
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:53,906
A favourite proof.

62
00:04:54,233 --> 00:04:58,234
De Moivre on one side, the binomial theorem on the other.

63
00:04:58,467 --> 00:05:02,701
Match real parts, and replace s squared by one minus c squared.

64
00:05:02,933 --> 00:05:07,724
Match imaginary parts, and replace c squared by one minus s squared.

65
00:05:09,733 --> 00:05:12,378
Roots are where De Moivre really shines.

66
00:05:12,378 --> 00:05:20,802
A complex number has infinitely many arguments, theta plus any multiple of two pi, and they all describe the same point.

67
00:05:20,802 --> 00:05:24,956
Take the n th root: divide each argument by n.

68
00:05:24,956 --> 00:05:26,232
Now they are different!

69
00:05:26,232 --> 00:05:32,821
The roots all have modulus r to the one over n, and are spaced two pi over n apart.

70
00:05:32,821 --> 00:05:36,485
Exactly n of them, k from zero to n minus one.

71
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:46,623
Find the cube roots of eight i. Eight i has modulus eight and argument ninety degrees, plus any multiple of three sixty.

72
00:05:46,623 --> 00:05:59,097
Take the cube root: modulus two, and arguments ninety over three, four fifty over three and eight ten over three: thirty, one fifty and two seventy degrees.

73
00:05:59,097 --> 00:06:04,527
Root three plus i, minus root three plus i, and minus two i.

74
00:06:04,527 --> 00:06:06,611
A perfect equilateral triangle.

75
00:06:07,833 --> 00:06:11,741
Solve z to the four plus sixteen equals zero.

76
00:06:11,741 --> 00:06:16,493
Minus sixteen has modulus sixteen and argument one eighty.

77
00:06:16,493 --> 00:06:25,387
Fourth roots: modulus two, angles forty five, one thirty five, two twenty five and three fifteen.

78
00:06:25,387 --> 00:06:31,523
Plus or minus root two, plus or minus i root two: the corners of a square.

79
00:06:32,733 --> 00:06:37,318
Put z equal to one: the n th roots of unity.

80
00:06:37,318 --> 00:06:43,841
They sit on the unit circle at the corners of a regular n sided polygon, with one corner at one.

81
00:06:43,841 --> 00:06:49,349
Their sum is always zero, because they balance perfectly around the centre.

82
00:06:49,349 --> 00:06:52,631
Their product is minus one to the n plus one.

83
00:06:54,433 --> 00:06:55,776
Exam tip.

84
00:06:55,776 --> 00:07:03,263
Always write theta plus two k pi before you divide by n, and list k from zero to n minus one.

85
00:07:03,263 --> 00:07:09,463
Then check: the n roots of z to the n equals a constant always add up to zero.

86
00:07:11,433 --> 00:07:12,724
Pause and think.

87
00:07:12,724 --> 00:07:14,299
Minus one minus i.

88
00:07:14,299 --> 00:07:15,624
What is its principal argument?

89
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:24,976
Minus one thirty five degrees.

90
00:07:24,976 --> 00:07:31,720
It is in the third quadrant, and the principal value must lie between minus one eighty and one eighty.

91
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:38,331
Forty five is what a calculator gives, and two twenty five is outside the principal range.

92
00:07:42,667 --> 00:07:44,674
Put n equal to three.

93
00:07:44,674 --> 00:07:51,614
Factorise z cubed minus one: z minus one, times z squared plus z plus one.

94
00:07:51,614 --> 00:08:00,273
So one root is one, and the quadratic gives the other two: minus one plus or minus i root three, over two.

95
00:08:00,273 --> 00:08:01,809
Call the first omega.

96
00:08:01,809 --> 00:08:04,166
The other is omega squared.

97
00:08:04,166 --> 00:08:08,453
On the diagram, they are an equilateral triangle on the unit circle.

98
00:08:08,667 --> 00:08:10,439
The properties you will use.

99
00:08:10,439 --> 00:08:15,583
Omega cubed is one, so reduce any power modulo three.

100
00:08:15,583 --> 00:08:26,012
One plus omega plus omega squared is zero, the most useful line in the unit: so one plus omega is minus omega squared, and so on.

101
00:08:26,012 --> 00:08:31,038
Omega squared is the conjugate of omega and also its reciprocal.

102
00:08:31,038 --> 00:08:36,458
And they factorise a cubed plus b cubed plus c cubed minus three a b c.

103
00:08:37,667 --> 00:08:38,687
Reduce the powers.

104
00:08:38,933 --> 00:08:42,676
A hundred is three thirty three plus one: omega.

105
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:46,417
Two hundred leaves remainder two: omega squared.

106
00:08:46,417 --> 00:08:47,862
Three hundred: one.

107
00:08:48,067 --> 00:08:52,021
And omega plus omega squared plus one is zero.

108
00:08:54,033 --> 00:08:55,701
Never expand the cubes.

109
00:08:55,701 --> 00:09:02,910
Simplify each bracket first, using one plus omega plus omega squared equals zero.

110
00:09:03,133 --> 00:09:09,796
One plus omega is minus omega squared, so the first bracket is minus two omega squared.

111
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,921
Similarly the second is minus two omega.

112
00:09:13,133 --> 00:09:19,567
Cube them: minus eight omega to the six, which is minus eight, plus minus eight.

113
00:09:19,567 --> 00:09:20,669
Minus sixteen.

114
00:09:27,267 --> 00:09:30,185
Remember the exponential series from unit two.

115
00:09:30,185 --> 00:09:31,872
Put x equal to i theta.

116
00:09:32,100 --> 00:09:36,893
The powers of i cycle: i, minus one, minus i, one.

117
00:09:36,893 --> 00:09:39,901
Separate the terms with i from those without.

118
00:09:39,901 --> 00:09:45,042
The first bracket is the series for cos theta, the second for sin theta.

119
00:09:45,267 --> 00:09:50,568
So e to the i theta equals cos theta plus i sin theta.

120
00:09:50,568 --> 00:09:51,547
Euler's formula.

121
00:09:52,767 --> 00:09:57,599
As theta grows, e to the i theta walks round the unit circle.

122
00:09:57,599 --> 00:10:00,985
At theta equal to pi it reaches minus one.

123
00:10:00,985 --> 00:10:10,515
So e to the i pi plus one equals zero: Euler's identity, linking e, i, pi, one and zero in a single line.

124
00:10:10,733 --> 00:10:17,688
Every complex number is then r e to the i theta, and De Moivre is just the law of indices.

125
00:10:17,688 --> 00:10:26,149
Adding and subtracting e to the plus and minus i theta gives cos and sin as exponentials.

126
00:10:27,367 --> 00:10:28,486
Euler makes this short.

127
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:34,470
Two cos theta is e to the i theta plus e to the minus i theta.

128
00:10:34,470 --> 00:10:35,442
Cube both sides.

129
00:10:35,667 --> 00:10:42,895
Expand by the binomial theorem; the products like e to the i theta times e to the minus i theta are one.

130
00:10:42,895 --> 00:10:44,673
Each pair is twice a cosine.

131
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:45,738
Divide by eight.

132
00:10:48,133 --> 00:10:48,721
A classic.

133
00:10:49,367 --> 00:10:59,446
Multiply by x and solve the quadratic: x is cos theta plus or minus i sin theta, that is, e to the plus or minus i theta.

134
00:10:59,667 --> 00:11:09,160
Then x to the n plus its reciprocal is e to the i n theta plus e to the minus i n theta: two cos n theta.

135
00:11:12,367 --> 00:11:13,553
Pause and think.

136
00:11:13,553 --> 00:11:16,119
One plus omega plus omega squared.

137
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:23,783
Zero.

138
00:11:23,783 --> 00:11:33,770
They are the three roots of z cubed minus one, whose z squared coefficient is zero; geometrically, they balance round the origin.

139
00:11:33,770 --> 00:11:36,669
Three would be the answer only if omega were one.

140
00:11:38,667 --> 00:11:39,554
One more.

141
00:11:39,554 --> 00:11:40,938
One plus i to the eighth.

142
00:11:47,833 --> 00:11:48,785
Sixteen.

143
00:11:48,785 --> 00:11:57,881
Root two to the eighth is sixteen, and eight times forty five is three sixty degrees, which points along the positive real axis.

144
00:12:00,067 --> 00:12:01,123
Quick recap.

145
00:12:01,123 --> 00:12:06,696
Polar form is length and angle, with the angle taken from the plotted quadrant.

146
00:12:06,696 --> 00:12:10,511
Multiplying multiplies lengths and adds angles.

147
00:12:10,511 --> 00:12:13,246
De Moivre raises both to the n.

148
00:12:13,246 --> 00:12:18,259
The n th roots sit on a regular polygon and add to zero.

149
00:12:18,259 --> 00:12:21,970
One plus omega plus omega squared is zero.

150
00:12:21,970 --> 00:12:28,685
And Euler's formula says e to the i theta is cos theta plus i sin theta.

151
00:12:32,867 --> 00:12:34,626
That was complex numbers.

152
00:12:34,626 --> 00:12:42,218
Next, sequence and series: sums of squares and cubes, and proof by mathematical induction.

153
00:12:42,218 --> 00:12:42,747
See you there.

